WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.469
OK, just for
argument's sake, suppose

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.137
we're nothing more
than the sum total

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:06.740
of our genetic inheritance.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.742
It's not as bad as it sounds.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:13.179
There are passages in our DNA
that are every bit as heroic

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:16.049
as anything ever written
in any epic saga.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:18.017
[low growl]

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:19.018
[gentle music]

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.187
[birds chirp]

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.525
[ominous music]

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.528
[growl deepens]

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.868
[dramatic music]

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.173
The mother is stotting,
deliberately putting

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.910
her own life in danger
to alert the herd

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.145
and buy time for them--

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:12.138
so that her offspring and the
rest of the herd can get away.

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:15.408
If this isn't heroism, what is?

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:18.812
And yet, this act of valor
is encoded in the Thompson

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.547
gazelles programming.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:22.782
Does that make it
any less brave?

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:26.953
She risks being eaten in
order to save her kin,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.189
and that's the key.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.424
Kin selection is
that genetic impulse

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:34.828
for self-sacrifice prevalent
throughout the kingdoms

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.665
of life, even us.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.533
Skeptical?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.435
Then do this thought
experiment with me.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.308
Imagine trying to
sleep soundly at night

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.877
knowing that your
children are starving,

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.379
homeless, or gravely ill.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.083
For almost all of us,
this would be unthinkable.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.354
But 16,000 children
die each day of easily

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.525
preventable hunger,
neglect, or disease.

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:11.297
Children continue to die as we
sleep well and watch this show.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.166
They are far away.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:16.503
They're not directly
related to us.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:21.140
Now tell me you don't believe
in the reality of kin selection.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.209
We will die to
protect the carriers

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:26.112
of our DNA and turn
away from the suffering

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:28.214
of those who don't.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:32.352
Kin selection can inspire
us to die saving a brother,

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.587
but it's also the
drive exploited

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:38.324
by the demagogue
and the supremacist,

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:41.327
the us against them.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:43.796
[inspiring music]

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.735
Another thought
experiment-- imagine

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:52.372
you're making the first approach
to a newly discovered planet.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.541
You've used various
techniques to ascertain

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.610
that this world supports
an astonishing variety

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.212
of lifeforms.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.314
You want to make
contact, but not

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:03.316
with their version
of a scorpion,

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:06.853
cobra, or great white shark.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.857
You want to find the form that's
most likely to respond gently

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:12.725
with empathy and intelligence.

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:17.897
These macaque
monkeys are members

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:21.668
of what might be the most
compassionate species on Earth.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.938
This was demonstrated in a
ghastly series of experiments

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.440
conducted in the 1960s.

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:31.377
15 macaques were fed only
if they were willing to pull

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:35.214
a chain and electrically shock
an unrelated macaque whose

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:38.618
agony was in plain view
through a one-way mirror.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:43.489
If they refused to shock
another macaque, they starved.

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:46.125
After learning the ropes,
the monkeys frequently

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:48.294
refused to pull the chain.

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.630
In one experiment,
only a small percentage

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:54.901
would do so, while more than
2/3 preferred to go hungry.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:58.271
One macaque went without
food for nearly two weeks

00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:00.607
rather than hurt its fellows.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.209
Here's the part
that really gets me.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.345
Macaques, who had
themselves been shocked

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.347
in previous
experiments, were even

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:11.384
less willing to pull the chain.

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:14.220
Relative social status
or gender of the macaques

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:17.857
had little bearing on their
reluctance to hurt others.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:22.495
These experiments permit
us to glimpse in non-humans

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.860
a saintly willingness
to make sacrifices

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.320
in order to save others, even
those who are not close kin.

